# The Art of Fugue, BWV 1080: Contrapunctus 13, a 3, inversus (J. S. Bach)
# The three-voice mirror fugue, upside down: every voice of the rectus
# (AOF3A) with its intervals inverted. In the 1751 print and modern
# editions it is Contrapunctus 13 (XIII) inversus; Mutopia files it under
# "Contrapunctus XVI". D minor, 2/2, 71 bars, bar for bar with the rectus.
#
# The voices trade places in the mirror (teoria.com): the rectus alto
# becomes the inversus soprano, the rectus bass becomes the alto, and the
# rectus soprano becomes the bass. So every entry falls in the same bars
# as in the rectus, in a different voice, and the subject forms swap
# (rising triplets become falling ones and the other way round).
#
# Bars: as in the rectus, the score's quarter-note pickup is counted in
# the MIDI's bar 1, so bar N here starts on the last beat of score bar
# N-1, and each entry's upbeat lands on the first beat of its bar.
#
# Character and tempo: as for AOF3A. Half note = 60, about 2:25. The
# pianist timings found run a little longer for the inversus than the
# rectus (Cano Smit 2:18, Nosrati 2:18, half about 62); Hall's brass
# recording 2:16 for both. The pair plays back to back at one tempo.
#
# Structure (mirroring AOF3A; keys read from the MIDI, which is a tonal
# rather than an exact mirror, so the middle keys differ):
#   1-4    subject (inverted shape) in the soprano (top), alone
#   5-8    answer in the alto (mid); the bass has not entered, so the
#          alto is the lowest line here
#   9-12   second answer in the bass (bottom); cadence toward G minor at
#          12-13 (where the rectus reaches A)
#   13-19  episode: dotted arpeggio figures in sequence
#   20-23  entry in the soprano, D minor; cadence in D minor at 23
#   23-26  falling sequence
#   27-28  head in the bass, on C
#   29-32  entry in the alto, F major (the bass rests 31-32, so the alto
#          is the lowest line there)
#   33-36  entry in the bass, F major (the rectus is in B-flat here)
#   37-40  entry in the soprano, A minor, over a held E in the alto
#          (38-41: the rectus's bass pedal, mirrored into the alto)
#   41-47  episode toward the dominant
#   48-51  entry in the soprano, D minor
#   52-58  rising sequence in imitation
#   59     fermata on a diminished seventh chord, as in the rectus
#   60-61  running triplets, transition
#   62-65  entry in the alto, D minor
#   66-69  entry in the bass, D minor, under a held D in the alto
#   69-71  final cadence onto D major (F sharp in the soprano), fermata

tempo 60 2             # half note, the same as the rectus
voice mid 0            # as in the rectus, the alto carries entries too

dyn 1 mp               # bars 1-12: exposition, as in the rectus
lead 5-8 bottom        # bars 5-8: answer in the alto, the lowest line until the bass enters
lead 9-12 bottom       # bars 9-12: second answer in the bass

dyn 13 mf              # bars 13-26: episode and the soprano entry, as in the rectus
lead 20-23 top         # bars 20-23: entry in the soprano

dyn 27 f               # bars 27-40: the major-key middle, as in the rectus
lead 27-28 bottom      # bars 27-28: head in the bass
lead 29-30 mid         # bars 29-30: entry in the alto
lead 31-32 bottom      # bars 31-32: the alto goes on, now the lowest line (bass resting)
lead 33-36 bottom      # bars 33-36: entry in the bass, F major
lead 37-40 top         # bars 37-40: entry in the soprano, A minor

dyn 41 mp              # bars 41-47: episode toward the dominant, as in the rectus
dyn 48 mf              # bars 48-51: the tonic entry
lead 49-51 top         # bars 49-51: entry in the soprano (the alto runs close to it in
                       # bar 48, so the mark starts a bar late)

ramp 52-58 f           # bars 52-58: the rising sequence to the fermata
broaden 59 0.7         # bar 59: the fermata on the diminished seventh

dyn 60 mf              # bars 60-61: running transition
dyn 62 f               # bars 62-71: the last two entries, alto then bass
lead 62-64 mid         # bars 62-64: entry in the alto (the bass rests in 65, so stop)
lead 66-69 bottom      # bars 66-69: entry in the bass

rit 2 0.7              # last 2 bars into the D major chord and its fermata
